GROUP BY (Aggregation,Simple Data Analysis): Collapses multiple rows into a single summary row. 
If you group 100 rows by department, you get back only the number of departments (e.g., 5 rows). You lose access to the individual row-level details unless they are part of the grouping key.
Group level calcualtions,less granular
ORDER OF EXECUTION - After FROM and WHERE but before HAVING
UseCases - Totals per category, finding max/min per group, counting items.

SELECT author, SUM(lines_changed) AS total_lines
FROM commits
GROUP BY author;

Window Functions(Advanced Data Analysis): Computes aggregates across a set of rows while preserving the original rows. 
If you run a window function over 100 rows, you still get back 100 rows, but with an extra column containing the calculated result (like a running total or a moving average).
Row level calculations,more granular
ORDER OF EXECUTION - After GROUPBY and HAVING mostly in SELECT or ORDER BY
UseCases - Running totals, moving averages, ranking (ROW_NUMBER()), comparing a row to its previous row (LAG()).

SELECT 
    author, 
    commit_date, 
    lines_changed,
    SUM(lines_changed) OVER (PARTITION BY author ORDER BY commit_date) AS running_total
FROM commits;
